\subsection{Measurement-only vertical acceleration}
\label{sec:vertical-proxy}

The adaptive parameters must be driven by sea motion without feeding the MEKF
state back into its own scheduler.  The implementation therefore extracts a
levelled vertical acceleration with a lightweight Mahony complementary observer
\cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary} driven only by raw gyro and
accelerometer measurements,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\vct\varepsilon&=\hat{\vct d}_{\mathrm{meas}}\times\hat{\vct d}(\hat q),
\qquad
\hat{\vct d}_{\mathrm{meas}}=-\vct f^{B}/\lVert\vct f^{B}\rVert,\\
\dot{\hat q}&=\tfrac12\,\hat q\otimes
\bigl(\vct\omega^{B}+\vct\beta+k_P\vct\varepsilon\bigr),
\qquad
\dot{\vct\beta}=k_I\vct\varepsilon .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:proxy-mahony}
\end{equation}
The resulting up-positive acceleration is
\begin{equation}
a^{P}_{\uparrow,k}
=-\!\left(\left[\mat{R}_{NB}(\hat q_k)\vct f^{B}_k\right]_{D}+g\right).
\label{eq:proxy-vertical}
\end{equation}
Only proxy tilt is used; yaw is irrelevant to the vertical channel.  The
default gains are $k_P=\SI{0.1}{\per\second}$ and
$k_I=\SI{0.01}{\per\second\squared}$ (the source stores $2k_P,2k_I$).  Its
correction corner lies below the wave band, so gyro propagation carries
wave-frequency attitude while accelerometer feedback trims slow tilt and the
integral term removes standing gyro-bias tilt.  The same observer later
supplies startup tilt and the yaw-stripped magnetic-reference frame in
Sec.~\ref{sec:startup-policy}.

MEKF-based levelling would close a loop from attitude error through the tuner;
unlevelled body-$Z$ remains exogenous but suffers strongly integrated gravity
leakage.  The proxy therefore provides both levelling and the one-way
measurement-only information flow required by the source-reachable scheduling
argument of Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-source-family}.  The MEKF-levelled alternative is
retained only for ablation.

\subsection{Noise-corrected vertical acceleration scale}
\label{sec:adaptation-physical-sigma}

The same vertical channel supplies the physical wave-band acceleration
statistic $\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$.  The point of the statistic is to estimate the
amplitude of physical wave excitation, not the electronics-noise amplitude and
not the OU-prior variance.  Before its variance is accumulated,
$a^{P}_{\uparrow}$ passes through a period-scaled wave band whose corners are
fixed relative to the lagged tuning frequency.  The period estimator defining
that frequency is given next; using the lagged value keeps the current sample
from selecting its own band.

The implementation first clips the supplied tuning frequency to the tuner's
local frequency bounds, then forms a sea-time base and applies the common
dynamic-time guard,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f_{\mathrm{eff}}&=\clip(f_{\mathrm{tune}},\SI{0.05}{Hz},\SI{5}{Hz}),\\
T_{\mathrm{sea,var}}&=\clip\!\left(\frac{1}{2f_{\mathrm{eff}}},
 \SI{0.5}{s},\SI{6}{s}\right),\\
T_{\mathrm{eff}}&=2T_{\mathrm{sea,var}},\\
\tau_{\mathrm{var,req}}&=\clip\!\left(K_{\mathrm{periods}}T_{\mathrm{eff}},
 \SI{0.3}{s},\SI{60}{s}\right),\\
\tau_{\mathrm{var}}&=\clip\!\left(\tau_{\mathrm{var,req}},
 \SI{0.05}{s},\SI{30}{s}\right),\\
\alpha_{\mathrm{var}}&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{var}}}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sigma-variance-horizon}
\end{equation}
The implementation uses $K_{\mathrm{periods}}=4$, shared by all three filter
families.  Across the eight scored seas $T_z$ spans about
\SIrange{2.3}{8.4}{s}, so the requested horizon spans about
\SIrange{9}{34}{s}; the final \SI{30}{s} guard therefore binds on the largest
sea.  This is an implemented saturation, not an asymptotic approximation.

The band-limited first and second raw moments are maintained by two identically
weighted, debiased exponential accumulators.  For either raw moment $u_k$ the
stored numerator and weight obey
\begin{equation}
 n_k=(1-\alpha_{\mathrm{var}})n_{k-1}+\alpha_{\mathrm{var}}u_k,
 \qquad
 w_k=(1-\alpha_{\mathrm{var}})w_{k-1}+\alpha_{\mathrm{var}},
\end{equation}
and the reported moment is $n_k/w_k$.  The variance is the difference between
the debiased second raw moment and the square of the debiased first moment.
There is no second EWMA of the already-smoothed variance, so
$\tau_{\mathrm{var}}$ is the complete variance-memory time constant.

Because a variance estimate from the measured signal contains both physical
wave energy and sensor noise, using it directly would impose a nonzero apparent
sea amplitude even in still water and would make the adaptive amplitude depend
on the sensor rather than on the sea.  The band-referred sensor floor is
therefore removed in variance units before taking a square root,
\begin{equation}
\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2
=\max\!\left(0,
 \widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{tot}}^2-\sigma_{\mathrm{nf,band}}^2\right),
\qquad
\widehat\sigma_{a,B}:=\sqrt{\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2}.
\label{eq:sigma-noise-subtraction}
\end{equation}
This subtraction is necessary because independent variances add; subtracting
standard deviations would not be statistically consistent.

The shared low-motion detector adds one implementation-specific exponential
path that is deliberately separate from the sea-scaled estimator and scheduler
EMAs above.  On the low-pass acceleration-band tracker input it maintains
\begin{equation}
 E_k=0.95E_{k-1}+0.05\left(a_{\uparrow,k}^{\rm LP}/g\right)^2,
 \label{eq:stillness-energy-ema}
\end{equation}
and declares stillness while $E_k<8\times10^{-4}$.  This is a fixed-gain
sample-domain EMA; it is not parameterized by $T_{\rm sea}$ and should not be
confused with Eqs.~\eqref{eq:sigma-variance-horizon} or
\eqref{eq:adapt-ewma}.  Once still, the code attenuates the noise-corrected wave
variance by the time-domain envelope
\begin{equation}
 \widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2
 \leftarrow
 e^{-t_{\rm still}/\SI{1}{s}}\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2
 \label{eq:stillness-wave-variance-decay}
\end{equation}
before the nonnegative floor and square root are applied.  The same detector
also relaxes the separate acceleration-band carrier toward its target after
\SI{2}{s} of stillness with a \SI{1}{s} time constant.  That relaxed carrier is
used for direction/reporting; it is not the wave-period tuning frequency, which
continues to come from the canonical estimator below.  OU--II uses the same
shared detector and variance attenuation path.

The nominal band corners are
\begin{equation}
f_L=0.5f_{\mathrm{tune}},
\qquad
f_H=4f_{\mathrm{tune}},
\label{eq:implemented-scaled-band}
\end{equation}
with absolute safety clamps of \SI{0.01}{Hz} and \SI{6}{Hz}, plus a Nyquist
guard.  Away from the clamps, the band shape is fixed in normalized frequency.
For the corresponding two-state time-varying band filter, the implementation
propagates the unit-input covariance
\begin{equation}
\mat P_{B,k}
=\mat A_{B,k}\mat P_{B,k-1}\mat A_{B,k}^{\top}
 +\vct b_{B,k}\vct b_{B,k}^{\top},
\label{eq:sigma-band-noise-covariance}
\end{equation}
and uses
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{\mathrm{nf},B,k}^2
=\sigma_{\mathrm{nf}}^2[\mat P_{B,k}]_{22}
\label{eq:sigma-band-noise-floor}
\end{equation}
as the band-referred sensor floor.

\subsection{Wave-period estimate and sea time scale}
\label{sec:wave-band-period}

The wave-direction acceleration carrier is not the tuning frequency.  Because
acceleration weights elevation spectra by $\omega^4$, that carrier remains near
the upper energetic band as sea period grows.  The tuner instead estimates the
elevation zero-crossing period $T_z=2\pi\sqrt{m_0/m_2}$ from the same exogenous
vertical channel.

The levelled acceleration $a^{P}_{\uparrow}$ passes through two high-pass stages
and two leaky integrators with common corner $\lambda$, producing velocity and
elevation proxies $v_p$ and $\eta_p$.  Their common filtering cancels in the
ratio, so for a narrow band
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sigma_{v_p}}{\sigma_{\eta_p}}=\sqrt{\omega^{2}+\lambda^{2}},
\qquad
\widehat{\omega}^{2}
=\frac{\sigma_{v_p}^{2}}{\sigma_{\eta_p}^{2}}-\lambda^{2},
\qquad
\widehat{T}_{z,\mathrm{raw}}=\frac{2\pi}{\widehat{\omega}} .
\label{eq:wave-band-period}
\end{equation}
The high-pass stages suppress very-low-frequency strapdown leakage without
changing this ratio; no MEKF position, velocity, attitude, or covariance is
used.

The two proxy variances in this ratio are themselves exponentially weighted
statistics.  After the leaky-integrator settling interval $6/\lambda$, the
velocity and elevation first and second moments use the same debiased EWMA
weight with
\begin{equation}
\tau_{\mathrm{mom}}
 =\clip\!\left(4T_{z,\mathrm{can}},\SI{20}{s},\SI{180}{s}\right),
\qquad
\alpha_{\mathrm{mom}}=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{mom}}}.
\label{eq:wave-period-moment-horizon}
\end{equation}
Before a canonical period exists, the source uses a \SI{6}{s} period fallback,
so the initial requested moment horizon is \SI{24}{s}.  As in the acceleration
variance channel, the stored first and second moments are debiased by a common
EW weight; there is no second smoothing stage on either resulting variance.

The canonical period is not an arithmetic EMA of frequency.  The first valid
raw period initializes the canonical log-period directly.  Subsequent samples
update
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\ell_k&:=\log T_{z,k},\\
\tau_{\ell,k}&=\clip\!\left(0.05e^{\ell_{k-1}},
 \SI{0.05}{s},\SI{30}{s}\right),\\
\alpha_{\ell,k}&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\ell,k}},\\
\ell_k&=\ell_{k-1}
 +\alpha_{\ell,k}\left(\log \widehat T_{z,\mathrm{raw},k}-\ell_{k-1}\right).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:tuning-log-period-horizon}
\end{equation}
The reported period and tuning frequency are exact reciprocals of this one
canonical state,
\begin{equation}
\widehat T_z=e^{\ell},\qquad f_{\mathrm{tune}}=e^{-\ell}.
\label{eq:tuning-period-reciprocal}
\end{equation}
Thus the numerical horizon equals $0.05T_z=0.10T_{\mathrm{sea}}$, but the
implemented state is log period, not a bounded EMA of $1/T_z$.  This smoother
uses the universal final horizon guard only; the separate
$[\SI{0.5}{s},\SI{6}{s}]$ dynamic time-base guard is applied later when a
consumer turns the estimated sea time into its own EMA horizon.  Before the
first valid period, the scheduler uses the fixed bounded wave-band prior.